                Preface

In the journey of success, the adventures and experiences along the path
are oftentimes more beneficial and vital than the perks of arrival at the
destination. Thus, in the quest for success, the attributes and attitudes
one develops and what one becomes due to the demands of the journey
are more profound, lasting and beneficial than the things one acquires
for getting to the destination.

Like all journeys to the unknown, the value of a tour guide in the
journey of life is immeasurable. Wisdom is the principal guide in the
journey of life. At every point, Wisdom tells what to do, which way to
go, where to take a bend, when to halt, with whom to and not to walk,
and guides on many other crucial decisions in the course of the journey.
Wisdom prevents from errors thereby increasing one’s chances of safe
arrival to destiny.

The Wisdom of God is the foundation upon which the entire universe is
built. Therefore, access to Divine Wisdom is like having the architect
who designed the plans of a very complex mansion to show one around
the building. One cannot miss the way when the designer and the
blueprint is also the guide.

This book is a compilation of Wisdom, especially as it relates to making
a success out of your life, vision and destiny. The principles are simple
and profound. They are also based on the God’s Word. The principles
communicated in this book will encourage, challenge, inspire and
enlighten you to become all you could.
                                 CHAPTER 1

            THE CONCEPT OF TIMES
                    AND SEASONS

God lives in the realm of eternity but created man to live in the realm of
time. One display of God’s supremacy over man is His choosing to live
in eternity and limiting man’s earthly life to time. Therefore, no matter
how powerful a human is, the fact that his/her life on earth is subject to
time is already an imposed limitation. Time is one of the most profound
mysteries of life. Knowing how to relate correctly with time is one of the
greatest displays of wisdom. In fact, an urgent consciousness of time in
daily life is one of the hallmarks of wisdom. Thus, Psa. 90:12 says, teach
us to realize the brevity of life, so that we may grow in wisdom (NLT).

The Creation of Time

Eternity is beyond the scope of our understanding, so it is futile
attempting to explain it. Eternity has no beginning but time does. It was
at a point along the unveiling of eternity that God decided to create
time. In Gen 1:14, God commanded lights to appear in the sky and to
separate day from night and to show the time for seasons, special days,
and years (CEV). Thus, time was created on earth and all earthly
creatures including man were subject to time. Time is a drop in the
ocean of eternity. Imagine a tadpole put in a closed cup, with all the
food it needs throughout its short lifespan, and the cup then thrown
into an ocean. Asking that tadpole to explain the length, breath, depth,
dynamics, physics and chemistry of the ocean is a simpler task than a
man within time trying to wrap his mind around explaining eternity and

                                                               1
                                        TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

God who fills all eternity.
Trying to subject God to man’s limited understanding or saying God
does not exist because one cannot reason Him out is the peak of
foolishness. God lives in eternity, we live in time. Time is an atom in
the ocean of eternity.

Any measurable time, no matter how large is a grain in the sand dune of
eternity. So, a billion or a zillion years is still nothing in the context of
eternity. The human mind cannot fathom or explain the Eternal God.
This is the reason the Bible says, only fools say in their hearts "There is
no God." . . . The Lord looks down from heaven on the entire human race;
He looks to see if anyone is truly wise, if anyone seeks God (Psa.14:1-2).
It truly takes a fool to conclude that God does not exist because he
cannot explain Him within his limited frame of mind and time. God
looks down from the realm of eternity into time to see if there is anyone
who is wise enough to know that He exists beyond time and cannot be
completely described or subjected within the frame of time.

Understanding Seasons

In Gen 1:14, God commanded lights to appear in the sky and to separate
day from night and to show the time for seasons, special days, and years.
One of the reasons God created time is in order to mark seasons.
Therefore, time is a subset of eternity and seasons are subsets of time.
God created time because He wanted life on earth to be in seasons.
Having Seasons on earth is a display of God’s wisdom. ‘Seasons’ is the
real beauty of time. Imagine one’s entire life time is one long day a long
seventy-, eighty- or hundred and something years, spent without day or

                                                               2
                                THE CONCEPT OF TIMES AND SEASONS

Monday or Tuesday, January or December but just one long stretch of
time. Imagine you are born, go to school, work, marry, die and do
everything on earth within a long continuum of a day without the
seasonal coming of night and day, a week after another, month after
month or year after year. How would life be? How do you measure
progress? How do you rest?

Every day, week, month, and year is organized into seasons for the
purpose of order. Day time and night time are seasons of the day.
Morning, Afternoon, Evening and Night are seasons within the day.
Each day is a season within a week. Each week is a new season within
the month. Every New Year is a new season. A year also has different
climatic seasons depending on the part of the world. Temperate regions
have Autumn, Spring, Winter and Fall. Tropical regions have Rainy
Season, Dry Season, and Harmattan. Farm-life has planting seasons,
growing seasons, harvest seasons. Each Decade and Millennium is the
introduction of a new season.
Even events in life usher new seasons - graduating out of school, getting
married, giving birth to children starting a business or career all usher
us into new seasons. A new political government brings a state or
territory into a new season and so many other examples that depict
change in seasons.

The power of seasons lies in the fact that each new season always brings
a new hope, opportunity and possibility. Each day we wake with a
renewed hope to achieve what we could not the day before. If life on
earth was not programmed to be in daily seasons, one major negative
effect would be hopelessness and exhaustion. Life would be a too long
and tiring journey to the grave. One purpose of seasons is to renew
hope; the hope of change, the hope of possibility, the hope of a fresh
start and the hope of newness.

                                                              3
                                        TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

One purpose of seasons is to renew hope; the hope of change, the hope
of possibility, the hope of a fresh start and the hope of newness.
The scripture relates hope to daily renewal of God’s mercies. It says,
Yet, I still dare to hope when I remember this: The faithful love of the
Lord never ends! His mercies never cease. Great is his faithfulness; His
mercies begin afresh each morning (Lam 3: 21 - 23). Seasons are God’s
wisdom for new beginnings. This is the reason people make new
resolutions, goals and targets every New Year. It is the power of season
at work. When you fully understand TIMES AND SEASONS OF
LIFE as the book will teach, you will not wait for new years to bring
new seasons into your life, you will know how to appoint seasons to
yourself.
Life is all about seasons. Living a great life is all about maximizing each
season of life. A common mistake in life is people wishing and praying
for a great life while neglecting to greatly utilize the season of life they
are in. A great live is simply a sum total of greatness of each season of
life.

The Season for a Purpose and the Purpose of a Season

Understanding seasons and knowing the purpose for each season is the
domain of the wise. Eccl 3:1 says, to everything there is a season, a time
for every purpose under heaven (NKJV). This means that to get life right,
there is a season to do everything you are to do. Doing things out of
season is one of the greatest reasons for failure in life. The question of
life is not ‘will I be successful and great?’ The real question of life is ‘Am
I doing what I should be doing now so that I can have a life of success
and greatness? Prov. 10:5 says He who gathers in summer is a wise son;
He who sleeps in harvest is a son who causes shame. This scripture is not
portraying sleep as bad, rather, it is stating that sleeping at a season
when you should be working will lead to shame. The person that does
not plant during planting season will have nothing to take home during
harvest season.

                                                               4
                              THE CONCEPT OF TIMES AND SEASONS

Wisdom is knowing what to do at each season of your life.
There is a season for every purpose and there is a purpose for season.
It is equally great wisdom to learn to discern the purpose of the
seasons of your life. Whatever you are going through right now, good
or bad, when you discern correctly the purpose of the season, you
cooperate maximally with it and make maximum use of it. You may
be going through a not-so-desirable season in your life but until you
discover the purpose for it and co-operate with the purpose, you may
be stuck in it. Sometimes, there are lessons to pick, character and
attitudes to develop, changes in thinking and lifestyle to imbibe,
and/or skills to come out of difficult season. If you do not understand
the purpose of the season but keep complaining about the season, the
struggle continues until one understands the purpose for the season
and does what they are supposed to do.
You may also be going through a great season in life, have you
discovered the reason for the season? When the reason for a season is
not known, the season is wasted. Wisdom is knowing what to do at
each season of your life.

In Gen 41, Pharaoh had a dream which Joseph interpreted to mean
that there would be a season of abundance for seven years in Egypt
followed by a season of famine for seven years. Joseph further
advised that the purpose of the season of abundance was to save up
for the season of dearth. The truth is that whether this counsel was
taken or not, the seasons of abundance and famine would have come
all the same. It took a man of wisdom to make them understand the
purpose of the season of abundance. According to Gen41: 53 - 54, At
last the seven years of bumper crops throughout the land of Egypt came
to an end. Then the seven years of famine began, just as Joseph had
predicted. The famine also struck all the surrounding countries, but
throughout Egypt there was plenty of food.

                                                             5
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

The preservation of an entire civilization was hinged on their
understanding the purpose of a season. When people do not
understand TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE, they suffer and
struggle at best and die at the very worst.
There have been people who had a season of abundance in their lives
but because they did not know the purpose of the season, they
squandered the season and never recovered from the loss. It is
wisdom to know the season for every purpose and activity in your life
and it is even greater wisdom to know the purpose for every season of
your life.

                                                             6
                                 CHAPTER 2

                    The Phases of Life

A life time is too brief for one to achieve all its potentials and
possibilities. This is the reason we must treat life with a sense of urgency
in achieving our goal. As the scripture says, teach us to realize the
brevity of life, so that we may grow in wisdom. Obviously, that there, is
a strong relationship between understanding the Times and Seasons of
life and wisdom. Furthermore, your use of time is a direct measure of
how much wisdom you possess.

The Phases of Life

Life is in phases and we must maximize every phase of life so that our
overall life-journey results in success. It is our understanding of the
phases of life that makes us know what we are supposed to do per time
in life. 1 Chron. 12:32 talks about the men of Issachar, who understood
the times and knew what Israel should do -200 chiefs, with all their
relatives under their command (NIV). When people understand times
and Seasons, they know what they should do at every time and they
become commanders in life.

The phases of life as described below are idealistic. First, it is based on a
lifetime of 100 years and we know that many people do not live up to a
hundred. Secondly, it divides a life time into four even parts; and, we all
know that life is not even. A phase of life might stretch longer than
evaluated for one person and be less than evaluated for another. Two
people born on the same day will not die on the same day and will not

                                                               7
                                        TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

die on the same day and will not achieve milestones the same time. This
is the reason life is not to be lived in comparing ourselves, achievement,
time or progress against another person or a general standard. This
would create undue pressure and a sense of ingratitude for life if one is
behind the abstract standard or a sense of pride in some of people who
believe their achievement is in phase with the standard. Bible says that
self-comparison with another person is not wise. Thus, 2 Cor. 10:12
says, for we will not make comparison of ourselves with some of those who
say good things about themselves: but these, measuring themselves by
themselves, and making comparison of themselves with themselves, are not
wise. (BBE)

The intent and advantage of the model is that it create understanding as
to what one ought to focus on per time in life and helps one to keep an
entire lifetime in view in daily life. Thus, be grateful to God for where
your life is right now as you keep working diligently towards life
progress, not because you are racing against time-phases of achievement
set by man but because you are urgent towards fulfilling your purpose
and destiny in life.

The phases of life are:

1. The Learning/Formative Phase of Life: This is the time when value is
acquired, developed and mastered. At this phase, traits, skills and
discipline for success are learnt and built up. This is the time to invest,
make sacrifices and delay gratification. It is absolutely unnecessary to
be seeking to harvest all the fruits of one’s investment at this time. This
is also the phase where one should make all the mistakes and learn in
life.
Like the fractured bone of a child heals faster than that of adults,
mistakes made during the formative phase of life are easier to correct
than the ones made later.

                                                               8
                                               THE PHASES OF LIFE

Life coaches put this phase for the ideal life at age 0 to 25 years. This
time range is fixed not to mean that once one has passed this age, the
person cannot learn to position for success anymore. The time fixation
is to create a sense of urgency. Indeed, if one has past this age and has
not attained mastery in what one intends to be great at, one is running
behind schedule and needs to be more urgent about developing the
values needed for success. Subsequently, we will liken this phase to the
morning time of life.

2. The Earning/Deployment Phase of Life: After value has been
developed in the learning phase, it needs to be deployed for rewards of
success. At this phase of life, one should necessarily be dispensing his
gifts to his generation and should have started living one’s dreams.
Moreover, rewards of success, at different levels, should have started
coming in. Nonetheless, this is not the time to stop learning and
investing into oneself, it is time to even improve on what one does on
the job and no longer in the dream-room. Some level of results must be
evident in this phase. At the culmination of this phase, one should seek
to attain his highest life potential. Success coaches have set this phase at
age 25 – 50 years. As earlier said, if at fifty a person has not attained the
fullest potential, it does not mean such a person is undone, it does mean
that one should live with a greater sense of urgency though. In this
book, we liken this phase to the afternoon of life.

3. The Legacy Phase of Life: This is the time to be more intentional in
raising successors and developing a system for the perpetuation of one’s
impact by the next generation. It is wise to shift one’s focus from
investing more into oneself to investing into others that will continue
the legacy. At this age, you should have raised people that are the
extension of your greatness and who will continue to deploy your value
even when you are no more.

The expected age-range for the legacy phase of life is from 51 to 75
years. People in this age range that cannot point to other people they
have raised into greatness may be in for failure in the long-run even if
they succeeded in their personal lives. We shall refer to this as the
evening of life.

                                                               9
                                        TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

3. The Retirement Phase of Life. This is the time to hand over to the
next generation and be coaching from the sidelines. If death comes any
time between 76 to 100 years, it should meet one fulfilled and having
lived a full life. This is the night of life.
It is important to re-emphasize that the above categorization is not
based on scriptures but life-coaches simply use this to give a greater
understanding of life and what should be done per time. So, do not get
into a needless race against time that you must do something by
sometime by all means or feel defeated because you are at a certain age
and certain things have not happened. A sense of urgency in life is
different from a sense of desperation.
The main point of this chapter is for you to remember that life is brief
and there should be things we ought to be doing and attaining
progressively per time in life. So, we need to be even more deliberate
and live with a greater sense of urgency than portrait in the ideal
stratification shown above.

                                                              10
                                 CHAPTER 3

        The Life-Time and the Times of Life

God in His Wisdom created life to be in seasons because anything
short of that would create a tiring life of hopelessness. Our advantage
in life is in understanding the seasons of life - knowing what each
season is meant for and using each season accordingly.
Every day has different seasons - the morning, the afternoon, the
evening and the night. To have a great day, one must maximize all the
seasons of the day. Thus, knowing what to do in the morning, how to
maximize the afternoon, what is suited for the evening, and what is best
done in the night, makes an entire day profitable.
For example, it is important to create time to seek God in the morning
and spiritually order the day aright. It is also vital to have a time carved
out of every day when you feed your spirit, soul and mind with
empowering information through books, messages, speeches or videos
that fuel your drive towards your life goals. There should be ample time
every day for productivity and working at things that moves you
forward in your vision, career, and finances. Thus, work time is vital in
every day.
Additionally, there should also be a part of the day, which has to be
created, that you spend alone with yourself for personal reflection,
planning, thinking, envisioning and meditation.

                                                              11
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

Your day must have a time to exchange love with other people; give
and receive love from family, friends and acquaintances. There
should be a time for rest in each day. There should also be time
carved out for impacting the world of people around you.
Community outreach, getting involved in a ministry, serving in a
church unit, being part of a social/humanitarian endeavor must have
an allocated time in your life no matter how busy you are.
While touching lives and reaching out in impact to others should be
ingrained in your everyday life, carrying out outreaches to others,
getting involved in ministry service and other organized impact
endeavors may not be every day for someone whose primary work is
not related to such.

However, thinking one is too busy to have time to do something that
involves serving God or touching lives around is a deception. The
truth is that life is always busy, people must learn to create time for
the priorities and what must be done.
The reason some people live an entire lifetime, absorbed in self,
meeting up with the demands of life and having no one impacted as a
legacy that they lived is because they do not understand that time for
service to humanity, ministry and impact must be calculated into life,
no matter how busy you are.

Part of wisdom for living is knowing that time to make impact on
others, serve God or be part of an organized impact system. It is not
wisdom to be above thirty years old and there are not people who
can attribute something in the making of their lives to the impact of
your life - and these people should not be your immediate family
alone.

                                                            12
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

The Times of Life

There are seasons within a day and there are the seasons within a
lifetime. As obtained with the times of the day, there is also the
morning, afternoon, evening and night of life. Wisdom is knowing
and doing what each time of life is meant for. Eph. 5:15-17 expounds,
see then that ye walk circumspectly, not as fools, but as wise, redeeming
the time, because the days are evil. Wherefore be ye not unwise, but
understanding what the will of the Lord is.
It is obvious, once again, that there is a strong relationship between
wisdom and usage of time. Every day of life have been programmed
by default to go wrong by the ‘Fall of man’, it is your duty to make
sure your own day goes right. Unless you seize the times of the day by
the horns and make it yield what it needs to deliver to you, it will slip
through the hands and time will be going without it making a
commensurate contribution to your life progress.

Eph. 5:15-17 NLT says, so, be careful how you live. Don't live like
fools, but like those who are wise. Make the most of every opportunity
in these evil days. Don't act thoughtlessly, but understand what the
Lord wants you to do. Redeeming the time involves taking advantage
of the opportunities every time presents to you, maximizing every
second of life and making sure no time is wasted.
There are opportunities that inherent in the morning of life, there are
those to be seized at noon. Wisdom makes you know what is best
reserved for the evening time and night of life hunting when they
should be resting. Postponing what should be done in the morning of
life to the noon is foolishness. Doing in the morning what is reserved
for the night is lack of wisdom. Wisdom is doing the right thing at the
right time.

                                                            13
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

A scriptural example of the understanding of what to do at each time
of life is shown in Gen. 49: 27. It says, "Benjamin is a ravenous wolf; in
the morning he shall devour the prey, and at night he shall divide the
spoil." Benjamin, like a wolf, understands that the morning is for
hunting prey and evening is for eating the meat. Lack of wisdom
makes some people to eat all the meat in the morning so they have
nothing to eat at night. This is the reason some have to still be
hunting when they should be resting.

The Times of Life

Just as the day, life could be partitioned into the morning, afternoon,
evening and night. Knowing what to do at every time of life is the
secret to greatness in life. The Bible uses the analogy of the day to
portray human lifetime, it says, for you, a thousand years are as a
passing day, as brief as a few night hours. You sweep people away like
dreams that disappear. They are like grass that springs up in the
morning. In the morning it blooms and flourishes, but by evening it is
dry and withered (Psa. 90:4 - 6). Figuratively, people are born and
they spring in the morning, grow in the noon, become dry in the
evening, and wither and die at night.

The Morning Life

The morning of life starts from the time of birth to the time of youth.
The morning of life orders the entire life just like the morning of the
day orders the entire day. Contemporary research has shown that
very successful people are people who wake up early and maximize
the wee hours of the day. Literally, great people wake up early and
work before others wake.

                                                            14
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

Entire books and focus groups have been created on the power of
maximizing the morning of each as one of the habits that set apart
highly effective people apart from mediocrities in life. Similarly,
exceptionally successful people in life are often those who get their
paths in life right early. Just as ample use of the morning of the day
determines how efficient and productive a day is, maximizing the
morning of life determines how great a life would be. In order words,
to have a great life, you must wake up to pursuing your destiny in life
early.

It is amazing when young people in their twenties finish school and
popular culture says they are about to start life. The question is ‘what
have they been doing all the while?’ ‘Playing life?’ It is deception for
someone in their twenties to think that are still in rehearsals for life.
No, you are already live on the stage of life! Your life is far gone, you
are already getting too old - the morning is over.

A Nigerian adage says ‘the morning is the entire day’. There are
variants of this same adage in most cultures and languages,
attempting to emphasize the fact that whatever needs to get done in a
day is best done early in the morning. Thus, whatever makes teens,
early twenties, or parents think that they are still in the nursery of life
is a big deception. The morning of life is when the manifestation of
how well and how far one will go in life has to start. A man/woman
that will be great starts manifesting greatness in the morning.

The essence of this is for young people, most especially, to quit
thinking they are children and to take life by the horns. Also, for
parents, to stop pampering and protecting grown-ups under their
roofs.

                                                            15
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

The dawn of life, which is the earliest part of the morning of life, is to
teach eternal values, principles and virtues that would be a compass
for a person throughout life. If this was well-done by parents and
guardian, they should trust God and what they have taught their
children enough to start allowing them step into their destinies.

Prov. 22:6 says, train up a child in the way he should go: and when he is
old, he will not depart from it. Childhood is the earliest stage of the
morning of life - the dawn of life. This is the time for training. It is
important to ingrain sound doctrine, character, godliness,
responsibility, and principles strongly into children between the ages
of zero to twelve; else, the entire subsequent efforts would be remedial
measures. However, people in their teens are to be exposed to
responsibility. A wise man once said, the concept of adolescent is
being used to excuse foolishness in many people. The truth about life
is that you are either a child or an adult, and you become an adult
when you are no longer a child. 1 Cor. 13:11-12 says, when I was a
child, I spoke and thought and reasoned as a child. But when I grew up,
I put away childish things. Thus, according to scriptures, you stop
being a child when you grow up. Some grow up in their teens, some in
their twenties, some in their forties, some never grow up.

Things to do in the morning life

1. Seek and Know God: Ps. 63:1 says O God, thou art my God; early
will I seek thee: my soul thirstiest for thee, my flesh longest for thee in a
dry and thirsty land, where no water is. Isa. 55:6 says, seek ye the Lord
while he may be found, call ye upon him while he is near. This suggests
that a time comes when it is more difficult to seek God if you have
not done so earlier.

                                                            16
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

Ask anyone in the noon and evening of their lives, they will tell you of
some many legitimate demands of life that makes them not to have as
much time for spiritual pursuits, retreats, programs, outreaches like
during the morning of their lives. The people who are spiritually
strong in the noon and evening of life are those that have developed a
reservoir of spiritual grace in the morning of life.

2. Discover your purpose and direction in life: Isa. 50:4 says the Lord
God hath given me the tongue of the learned, that I should know how
to speak a word in season to him that is weary: he waketh morning by
morning, he waketh mine ear to hear as the learned. God wants you
to hear every morning the instructions and guidance for the day. He
even more wants you to hear early in the morning of life the
instructions, guidance, and direction for your life. It is an untold
advantage when you discover your purpose early in life; it sets your
life on the right course and makes your decisions to be error free from
the start of life.

3. Build and Invest for the future: the morning of life is not the time
for consumption but for investment. This is the time to invest your
time, talents, resources and money into building for the future. Eccl.
10:16 - 17 says woe to thee, O land, when thy king is a child, and thy
princes eat in the morning! Blessed art thou, O land, when thy king is
the son of nobles, and thy princes eat in due season, for strength, and
not for drunkenness! Some people eat their future in the morning by
the luxuries they buy. The morning is not the time for rest; why not
delay gratification till evening? It is not wrong to eat some things but
it has to be in due season. There are due TIMES AND SEASONS
OF LIFE for eating certain things. Some people are struggling in
their marriages because they have ‘eaten’ all the sex they could in the
morning.

                                                            17
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

At noon and evening, their appetite has become insatiable. Some
have ‘eaten’ all the money and do not have anything laid up for the
evening. The morning is not for consumption, it is for investment.
If someone misses the opportunities meant for the morning of life,
such a person may end up merely trying to play catch-up throughout
life. While God’s mercy can locate anyone and still make something
out of their lives even if they discover God or get serious with life late,
the indisputable point is that God is best found in the morning of life
and life is better when it is gotten right early in life.

Often times, people are quick to mention some people who started
late in life and still succeeded. However, for every two people they use
as examples of people who did not come to God early in life and still
made it, there are 98 that did not get life early and never recovered
from the loss. We cannot base principles on the exemption but on the
rule.

A number of people are quick to point out how Abraham stepped out
into destiny at the age of seventy and still made it and thereby claim
that it does not matter the time one gets it right in destiny. It is
important to know that Abraham was an exemption in this regards
and not the rule. First, you may not live up to 175 years like
Abraham (Gen 25: 7). Meaning that, Abraham stepped into destiny
at seventy and had a hundred and five years to fulfill it. Secondly, for
one Abraham in the Bible that stepped out late, there are ninety-nine
heroes that stepped out early. So, let us quit building our theologies
based on the exemption which is a show of God’s sovereignty but on
the rule which is the show of God’s principles.

                                                            18
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

The Noon of Life

The noon of life is the time for establishment and dominion. This is
the time when you rise to the peak of what God wants you to do in
life. While the morning is the time for starting in life, the noon is the
time for building in life. Eccl. 11:6-7 says, plant your seed in the
morning and keep busy all afternoon, for you don't know if profit will
come from one activity or another—or maybe both. You should be
busy building a career, ministry, home, business, impact and God’s
Kingdom.

On a regular day, when you see someone sleeping in the afternoon,
they are either lazy or sick - except they worked night shifts.
However, the noon comes before the night in life so the one who
sleeps in the noon of life is not because he worked a night shift but
because he is planning to work a night shift. If you do not want to be
working when you should be resting and mentoring others, you
should work in the noon.

This is the time when your life work must be full-fledged. Jesus said I
must work the works of him that sent me, while it is day: the night
cometh, when no man can work (John 9:4). You should pursue life
with such urgency at the noon like that is all you have to fulfill your
destiny, purpose and goals. After the noon, diminishing returns may
overtake your physical strength and you may have to go at an easier
pace in life. So, run in your youth - the morning and the noon.
According to Lam.3:27, it is good for a man that he bear the yoke in
his youth.

                                                            19
                                       TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

Things to do at Noon

1. Take responsibility and become self-dependent: While a large part
of the morning is spent as being dependent on parents and guardian,
one should have learned self-reliance in the late morning of life and
become self-dependent in the noon. The noon is not the time to be
feeding on parents, friends and relatives. It is a time when your hands
should be sufficient for you. Deut. 33:7 shows the blessing of Judah:
saying, Hear, Lord, the voice of Judah, and bring him unto his people:
let his hands be sufficient for him; and be thou a help to him from his
enemies. By the time you get to the noon of life, your hands must be
sufficient for you and you should not be dependent on someone.

2. Get establishment in life and purpose: The noon is the time for
establishment. It is God’s will that one gets established early in life.
According to Psa. 46: 5, God is in the midst of her; she shall not be
moved: God shall help her and that right early. God wants you to be
established in your life assignment, vision, business, finances,
marriage and career early. By noon, you should have started all the
necessary areas of life and commit to building them. Why should you
wait till after retirement from a career to start a business? The
timeline is inappropriate and most people that do it fail at it. The
noon is the time to establish things; start that business in your noon,
even as a career person and build it along with your career. You
should retire from your job at the evening into the business you have
built alongside in the afternoon, not retire in the evening to start
business. Also, like someone said, ‘it is not cool to be raising kids
with your pension’ - establish your home early it is daytime of your
life.

                                                            20
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

3. Build your life: The noon is the strongest part of your life and it
should be spent building what you will do with your life. You will
discover at the dusk of life that it is what you spent your noontime
building that you will be able to point to eventually. Build your
family, business, ministry, career with your ‘noon’ strength. Don’t
hold back at noon; go all out with the strength of your youth to build
what you will leave as a legacy in every area of your life.

4. Achievement is for the noon: Largely, the noon time is supposed
to be the peak of productivity of the day. Having maximized the
morning, the noon should be the peak period of the day. There is
usually a slowing down of pace in the evening. The night is the time
for rest. However, if by afternoon one has still not gone far in the
work for the day, then the day has slipped off one’s hand.

The Evening and Night of Life

The strength of youth will not always be there. Part of wisdom is
living with a time consciousness. Therefore, the days of youth must be
maximized. Your youth largely talks about the morning and
afternoon of your life. The evening is the time to consolidate on the
achievement of the day and actualize whatever elongates beyond life
work hours. In natural life, we all have had reasons to stay back at
work after official closing hours and work into the evening because
the day is spent and the work is not completed. So, evening is not for
resignation, it is still for work. You should work in the evening as
long as your life targets are not yet met and your life’s fullest
potentials are not fulfilled. However, the truth is that once evening
comes, the tempo of work drops because of the exhaustion of the long
haul of the day.

                                                            21
                                      TIMES AND SEASONS OF LIFE

This is the reason it is important to do what is most important in the
day and invest the best part of your energy into what matters most
early in life. The strength for work in the evening is not the same as in
the day - the morning and noon. Furthermore, when the night comes,
it is time to sleep. This is the reason Jesus said, I must work the works
of him that sent me, while it is day: the night cometh, when no man can
work (John 9:4).

The Bible gives a classic description of what happens once the evening
and night of life catches up with a person. In Eccl. 12:1-5, it says, Don't
let the excitement of youth cause you to forget your Creator. Honor
him in your youth before you grow old and say, "Life is not pleasant
anymore." Remember him before the light of the sun, moon, and stars is
dim to your old eyes, and rain clouds continually darken your sky.
Remember him before your legs—the guards of your house—start to
tremble; and before your shoulders—the strong men—stoop. Remember
him before your teeth—your few remaining servants—stop grinding;
and before your eyes—the women looking through the windows—see
dimly. Remember him before the door to life's opportunities is closed
and the sound of work fades. Now you rise at the first chirping of the
birds, but then all their sounds will grow faint. Remember him before
you become fearful of falling and worry about danger in the streets;
before your hair turns white like an almond tree in bloom, and you drag
along without energy like a dying grass hopper, and the caper berry no
longer inspires sexual desire. Remember him before you near the grave,
your everlasting home, when the mourners will weep at your funeral
(NLT). Fools spend the evening carousing, average people spend the
evening resting, and wise people spend the evening planning for the
next day.

                                                            22
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

When you see people in their fifties, sixties and seventies still
gallivanting and rocking life as if they are still young - partying,
carousing with the girls and boys, and riding life in funfair, you have
met a great fool. No matter the achievement such people had in their
morning and nights, they are not planning for tomorrow.

There is a tomorrow in the time of life; your tomorrow is your next
generation - your children, legacy, and the future of what you have
spent your life building. When you see people in their fifties, sixties
and seventies retiring into a life of laxity, you have met an average
person. What to do in the evening is to build the next generation and
transfer the baton to them. Spend your noon raising and building
people as you also build structures. Spend the evening of your life,
pouring all the wealth of your wisdom and experience into the next
generation and make sure you transfer all your vision, wisdom, and
systems into the hands of younger people you have raised so that
when night comes and you go to sleep, you can be rest assured that
what you spent your entire life on will outlive you.

It is vanity to spend all your life building something that dies when
you die because, in a little while, the story of such a person would
sound the same with that of someone who never built. Every
worthwhile vision you have must stretch beyond your lifetime even
from inception. In other words, you must factor how every vision you
have now would thrive and grow after you are gone. As you build
your God-given vision, you must build the system to be able to
eventually do without you. Identify potential successors from the
noon of your life, build them along as you build the vision, invest in
the people and make them wiser, better and mightier than you.

                                                            23
                              THE LIFE-TIME AND THE TIMES OF LIFE

Plan succession and transfer the leadership to them in the evening.
Don’t let death take leadership from you and enforce it into an
unready system. It is better to transfer leadership to the next
generation and be around to mentor them on the job as a coach in the
evening before the night of your life.
Every wise person has younger people around him/her that he or she
is working with and building into their life assignments through his
vision. Anyone that does not have younger people from a younger
generation that he/she is intentionally and strategically building into
his vision is not being wise. Also, note that there is a difference
between using people and building them. A major difference is that
when you are building people, you are dealing with them as leaders
and seeing them as people who will eventually take your place and do
the things you are doing. When you are using people, you are seeing
them as people who will ever be under you so that they can help you
rise higher and higher. In building, the focus is the people - you see
yourself as existing for them. In using, focus is you - you see the
people as existing for you and your vision. It is also worthy of note
that some of the people you built may not continue in the system you
built but continue or start another system entirely, however, that
system is still an extension of you even if it does not necessarily bear
your name. It is important to know this because I have met leaders
who are reluctant to build people because they nurse the pain of
having built some people and those people did not use the building
for the legacy of the system that leader is seeking its legacy but went
elsewhere or started their own system. Those leaders with such
complaints are those who are building people quite alright but still
have the motive of ‘using’ people.
A true builder is not building men because he wants to be the one that
will carry his vision and legacy.

                                                            24
