Understanding the balance between predestination and choices clarifies
the topic of God’s will in matters - including marriage. The seemingly
complex subject of God’s marital will among Christians is simplified
when we understand how predestination and choices work together in
God’s operating system. ‘God’s will’ is not a marriage subject, basically,
it is a body of knowledge and operating system that should be
understood holistically. God has a will and predestination in His
assignment and purpose for each person, but it is never cast in stone
because anything that involves humans and involves will is given the
flexibility of adaption and change by God. So, when God already has a
task He wants to achieve, He creates and preordains people with the
qualities and attributes suited for the fulfillment of that role. Even in the
natural job market, each job role has a fit of qualities, skills and
attributes that are suited for it. This is the reason vacancies are
advertised and recruitment exercises are conducted because it is not one
single person that will be the fit for every needed role. So, choices and
considerations need to be made in filling that position. Similarly, God
creates many people who have the fit of attribute and qualities for each
Kingdom role. Each Kingdom role – including marital roles for children
of God - has a particular matrix of persons that God created to be
suited for each unique situation by divine preordination. The ones that
align by their choices are the ones that God will put in that role. Thus,
whether in life, ministry or marriage, there is a kind of person
predestined by God for a role (meaning that the qualities necessary for
each role are in God’s mind as He creates different people with this
predestined role in mind).

