However, there is no single person such that it is either that person or
nothing else works. We must understand that the specifics of each
person created by God is based on God’s unique assignment and
purposes for him/her but there is no one that is predestined to a specific
role by name.
Thus, thinking that there is a particular person predestined by name for
marriage for another person which if he/she misses, would have
eternally missed the will of God is gross misunderstanding. Truly, there
is a kind of person predestined for each person in marriage based on the
unique assignment and destiny God has for him/her but there is no
single person that must be it or no one else. Just like other Kingdom
roles, it is the person who aligns with a marital role based on mutual
choice among the people suited by God’s preordination that gets the
job. A curious mind would ask, ‘Well, what if more than one person
aligns?’ Do not worry, in the Kingdom, the roles are infinite and there is
a task for everyone - God has an assignment for every willing person.
Simply put, if more than one person aligns, God has a spouse for every
of His children - that is why choice comes into play.

Therefore, there is predestination in marriage but not in the context
which it is being erroneously preached that there is a particular person
God created for each one to marry which if one misses, the person has
missed the will of God. The actual truth is that there is a matrix of
attributes making the kind of person you should marry in order to fulfill
destiny. Obviously, people in ministry do not struggle to understand
that marrying a person not suited for the role of a minister’s spouse can
cause ministry failure.

