one who will be the betrayer. The person who, through his tendencies
and choices, partners with the possibility would be the one to commit
the treason. Satan, therefore, went around the disciples, trying them one
after the other, to see the one that he could use as a betrayer. Satan
nearly succeeded with Peter but he was always malleable and open to
correction so he always came back whenever he missed it. Satan found
the perfect fit for the role in Judas who already had greed and the love
of money rooted in his heart. God does not predestine evil for any
person. People by their tendencies and warped desires partner with a
predestined evil and become the perpetrator of it.

There is always a substitute should a predestined person refuse to align
his choice with the predestined purpose or end. Still on the disciples of
Jesus, God has predestined that there would be twelve apostles. The
number twelve has spiritual significance in God’s architecture and the
Heavenly Jerusalem, built from the foundation of the world, has twelve
foundations, with the names of twelve apostles on it. Thus, when Judas,
by his choice, fell out of predestination as a foundational apostle into
damnation through treachery, God amply substituted someone else into
the role. According to Acts 1:15-22, Peter stood up and addressed them,
"Brothers," he said, "the Scriptures had to be fulfilled concerning Judas,
who guided those who arrested Jesus. This was predicted long ago by the
Holy Spirit, speaking through King David. . . "This was written in the
book of Psalms, where it says, 'let his home become desolate, with no one
living in it.' It also says, 'Let someone else take his position.'

