to their good or bad works.) She was told, "Your older son will serve your
younger son." In the words of the Scriptures, "I loved Jacob, but I
rejected Esau." Are we saying, then, that God was unfair? Of course
not! We must remember that God’s preordination is still subject to the
tendencies and choices of the people involved. God is sovereign but
limits the expression of His sovereignty in individual lives to the context
of the power of their choices and will.

Reading through the eventual lives of Esau and Jacob, one would see
that God knew what He saw before He predicted that the older would
be less than the younger. Before their birth, God, through His eternal
eyes, had foreseen the tendencies of Esau to marry strange girls, adopt
the hunting profession of the surrounding unbelievers over the
shepherding work of his ancestors, value food, sex and physical
gratification over spiritual inheritance, despise his birthday and esteem
the covenant lightly. All his tendencies and choices released the forces
that made him lose out of the greatness that was available to him. If He
had taken the prophecy to heart and determined to exercise his choices
toward God and the covenant, the story would have ended differently.
As established already, predestination does not mean something is
bound to happen anyways. Every man is given a level of control over
his destiny by his choices. Positive predestination has to be followed by
closely aligning ones choice with what has been written. Negative
predestination could be averted by aligning one’s choices against it.
Remember, it is the end result that God has in mind in predestination
and not necessarily the person.

